Optimizing Knowledge Placement in Small Language Models: Combining Fine-Tuning and Retrieval-Augmented Generation
Sigal Shaked
Faculty of Computer and Information Sciences, Ben Gurion University of the Negev, Israel
shaksi@post.bgu.ac.il
Keywords:	Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG), Fine-Tuning (FT), Hybrid Language Models, Knowledge Partitioning.
Abstract:	We explore how small language models can best distribute knowledge between fine-tuned parameters and external retrieval mechanisms. Our hybrid approach fine-tunes on stable topics while retrieving dynamic facts at inference time. Using HotpotQA with topic-based partitions, the hybrid system outperforms fine-tuning-only and retrieval-only baselines in accuracy, while achieving a favourable balance between semantic richness and factual grounding. The results show that combining understanding (stable, learned reasoning) with remembering (retrieved, dynamic evidence) enables generalization to unseen domains—much like a person who searches for new information before applying prior knowledge to solve a novel problem.
. 
1	INTRODUCTION
Language models internalize knowledge through fine-tuning (FT) or access it dynamically via retrieval-augmented generation (RAG). FT yields fluent, context-aware reasoning but encodes knowledge as a static snapshot that becomes outdated. RAG provides adaptability through external evidence but depends on retrieval accuracy and, in generative settings, adds latency.
We hypothesize that optimal knowledge allocation arises when stable, reusable concepts are learned through fine-tuning, while transient or topic-specific facts are retrieved at runtime. This hybridization parallels human cognition—semantic memory for enduring understanding and episodic memory for context-bound detail (Tulving, 1972; McClelland et al., 1995).
Our contributions are threefold: 1) A hybrid FT + RAG framework explicitly dividing stable and dynamic knowledge; 2) A controlled evaluation on HotpotQA using topic-based partitions to emulate stability without temporal data; 3) Empirical evidence that hybridization improves accuracy and semantic alignment on dynamic topics, while balancing factual grounding and adaptability under topic-level distribution shifts.
2	BACKGROUND AND RELATED WORK
Fine-Tuning (FT). FT adapts pretrained models to specialized domains by updating parameters (Gururangan et al., 2020). It improves domain fluency and reasoning but struggles to incorporate new information without retraining and may suffer from catastrophic forgetting.
Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG). RAG decouples factual knowledge from model weights (Lewis et al., 2020). By retrieving supporting passages at inference time, it boosts factuality and interpretability (Izacard & Grave, 2021). Limitations include retrieval latency, sensitivity to index quality, and limited context length. RAG systems may be implemented in extractive settings, where answers are directly selected from retrieved passages, or in generative pipelines that synthesize responses conditioned on retrieved evidence.
Hybrid Systems. Recent work explores combining internal and external memory (Shi et al., 2024), emphasizing the complementary strengths of parametric and non-parametric memory. Fine-tuning captures generalizable patterns, while retrieval introduces freshness and adaptability. This echoes cognitive theories of complementary learning systems (McClelland et al., 1995), where models “understand” via parametric memory but “remember” via retrieval.
Additional Related Work. Generative retrieval-augmented pipelines such as RAG-Sequence and Fusion-in-Decoder (FiD) illustrate how integrating retrieval during answer generation allows the model to synthesize evidence from multiple documents, yielding more complete and accurate responses than extractive retrieval-reader methods (Lewis et al., 2020; Izacard & Grave, 2021). Building on this idea, Atlas is a large-scale retrieval-augmented model that achieved state-of-the-art few-shot performance on knowledge-intensive tasks – even outperforming a 540B-parameter closed-book model while using only an 11B parameter generator (Izacard et al., 2023). These advances exemplify a hybrid memory approach: by combining parametric knowledge in model weights with non-parametric external memory, RAG systems can boost factual accuracy and adaptability, allowing smaller models to rival much larger parametric-only LMs (Borgeaud et al., 2022).
While these models jointly optimize retrieval and generation, they do not explicitly study how knowledge should be partitioned between parametric and non-parametric memory under distribution shifts.
Our work builds on this line of research by explicitly studying knowledge placement: rather than jointly optimizing retrieval and generation, we investigate which knowledge should be internalized through fine-tuning and which should remain externally retrievable—across both extractive and generative retrieval settings—particularly under topic-level distribution shifts. 
3	DATASET AND PREPROCESSING
3.1	Dataset
We use the HotpotQA benchmark (Yang et al., 2018), comprising ≈113 K multi-hop QA pairs over Wikipedia. Each question is supported by two gold documents providing explicit reasoning evidence.
Our experiments employ the distractor-free version, retaining only supporting documents.
To model stability without timestamps, we applied a topic-based partition:
	The stable subset originates from the train split (~90 K QA pairs) and was used for fine-tuning.
	The dynamic subset derives from the dev split (~7.4 K QA pairs) after filtering out topics overlapping with the held-out test set.
This guarantees that dynamic topics are novel during evaluation.
Evaluation sets include:
	4248 stable, 775 dynamic, and 400 mixed questions.
	Roughly 4,500 stable documents and 1,200 dynamic documents.
Each question retains its two supporting Wikipedia passages.
The dataset partition and preprocessing workflow are summarized in Figure 1, which illustrates how the HotpotQA corpus was divided into topic-based Stable and Dynamic subsets. This split ensured zero topical overlap between stable and dynamic domains, thus creating a controlled simulation of evolving knowledge. 
3.2	Chunking and Indexing
All supporting documents were segmented into overlapping chunks to standardize retrieval granularity and minimize context fragmentation.
We used a chunk size of 768 tokens with an overlap of 16 tokens, which provided balanced coverage and retrieval precision.
Each chunk was embedded using sentence-transformers/all-mpnet-base-v2, chosen for its strong semantic retrieval performance on long-form text.
Embeddings were indexed using FAISS with cosine similarity for efficient top-k retrieval. This setup allowed the retriever to maintain coherence across paragraph boundaries while ensuring fast lookup in the hybrid RAG pipeline.
3.3	Base Model and Two-Stage Fine-Tuning
Model Backbone 
We base our experiments on Mistral-7B-Instruct-v0.2, a compact decoder-only transformer optimized for efficient autoregressive generation and instruction-following behavior. As a decoder-only architecture, Mistral is well suited for multi-hop question answering, where coherent reasoning and step-by-step generation are required. Despite its moderate parameter scale, the instruction-tuned variant exhibits strong linguistic and reasoning capabilities, making it an appropriate backbone for studying knowledge allocation between parametric memory and external retrieval in compact language models.
 
Figure 1: Knowledge Partitioning.
Two-Stage Fine-Tuning Strategy
We fine-tuned Mistral-7B-Instruct-v0.2 using a two-stage QLoRA pipeline designed to internalize stable knowledge and then align it with realistic retrieval-augmented reasoning.
Stage 1 — Document-Only Adaptation (Stable Knowledge)
The model was first trained on the stable corpus to absorb terminology, reasoning structure, and domain regularities. This phase established a parametric “understanding” of the domain without exposure to question–answer patterns. Training used QLoRA (r = 16, α = 32, dropout = 0.05), 4-bit NF4 quantization, bfloat16 compute, sequence length = 1,760, micro-batch = 64, gradient accumulation = 1, learning rate (2 × 10⁻⁴), AdamW 8-bit optimizer with a cosine schedule and 100 warm-up steps. Gradient checkpointing and flash attention reduced memory usage. 
Stage 2 — Supervised Fine-Tuning on QA + Context (Stable → Task)
For the second stage, we constructed a synthetic realistic training set combining the stable QA pairs with their top-3 most relevant passages retrieved by our own RAG system. This design simulates an actual retrieval-augmented QA workflow, forcing the model to learn how to integrate evidence snippets rather than memorize gold answers in isolation. The same QLoRA configuration was used, with the Stage-1 adapters reused and a smaller learning rate (1 × 10⁻⁴). The model learned to reason over retrieved facts while preserving the stable semantic base from Stage 1.  
3.4	Implementation and Reproducibility
Training was orchestrated via Axolotl, using JSONL datasets: docs_for_pretrain.jsonl (Stage 1) and docs_qa_with_context_ALPACA.jsonl (Stage 2). Both were pre-processed with packing, no padding to sequence length, and gradient checkpointing. The system prompt in Stage 2 encouraged balanced use of retrieved evidence and model knowledge.
All models share identical decoding parameters to ensure fair evaluation against the various baselines. The complete YAML configurations are available at:
https://github.com/sigal-shaked/Optimizing-Knowledge-Placement-in-Small-Language-Models.
The compact scale of the backbone model enables reproducible experimentation without reliance on large-scale infrastructure.
4	ARCHITECTURE AND SYSTEMS COMPARED
We evaluate four system variants: (i) an extractive retrieval-only baseline (RAG_extractive), designed to isolate grounding quality; (ii) a generative RAG baseline using a frozen Mistral-7B-Instruct model without fine-tuning (RAG_generative); (iii) a fine-tuned model without retrieval (FT-only); and (iv) the proposed hybrid system combining stable fine-tuning with dynamic retrieval. 
The training and inference flow of our suggested Hybrid FT + RAG system is visualized in Figure 2, which outlines the hybrid architecture where stable data informs parameter tuning while dynamic data remains retrievable at runtime.
RAG-extractive: Retrieves answers directly from an index containing both stable and dynamic documents, without any language-model generation. Serves as a pure retrieval baseline (Karpukhin et al., 2020). All retrieval operations relied on FAISS indexing with embeddings from all-mpnet-base-v2 for consistency across experiments. The RAG-extractive baseline is intentionally implemented as an extractive retrieval system, without generative language modelling.
RAG-generative (Frozen LM): This variant implements a standard generative RAG pipeline by combining retrieval with answer generation using a frozen language model. Retrieved passages are supplied as context to the base Mistral-7B-Instruct model, without any fine-tuning. This baseline evaluates the contribution of retrieval-augmented generation alone, isolating the effect of external memory while keeping the parametric knowledge fixed. Retrieval is performed over the full index (stable + dynamic documents) using FAISS and embeddings from sentence-transformers/all-mpnet-base-v2, ensuring consistency with other retrieval-based systems.
FT-only: A compact model fine-tuned solely on stable data; no retrieval at inference. Tests how well parametric memory alone supports QA.
Hybrid FT + RAG (ours): The retrieval component in the hybrid setup is restricted to the dynamic index and reuses the same FAISS index and embeddings from sentence-transformers/all-mpnet-base-v2, ensuring consistent retrieval quality across all systems. Retrieved snippets (top-3) were then provided as input to the fine-tuned Mistral model trained on stable knowledge.
 
Figure 2: Hybrid Training and Inference Pipeline.
5	EVALUATION SETUP
We assess model performance on three evaluation subsets—Stable, Dynamic, and Mixed—using a comprehensive suite of lexical, semantic, and factual metrics (Table 1). Stable questions are drawn from topics seen during fine-tuning, Dynamic questions cover previously unseen topics, and Mixed questions combine both settings. 
Unless stated otherwise, retrieval uses top-k = 5 passages, a common setting for multi-hop QA. We also observed that reducing k to 3 slightly degrades retrieval-based baselines, consistent with the increased difficulty of evidence coverage in multi-hop QA.
These metrics collectively capture both the linguistic quality and factual reliability of generated answers. Higher values generally indicate better performance, except for hallucination rate, where lower values are preferred.
To better interpret these metrics, we group them into three complementary dimensions:
Accuracy-Oriented Metrics (EM, F1, Semantic Similarity): These capture the factual and semantic alignment between the model's response and the ground truth. While Exact Match (EM) and F1 focus on exact or partial token overlap, semantic similarity allows for lexical variation, reflecting true meaning preservation.
Text Quality Metrics (BLEU, ROUGE-L, chrF): These evaluate the fluency, coherence, and lexical richness of responses. They are particularly useful for identifying outputs that are correct yet phrased awkwardly or unnaturally. BLEU is included for completeness, but is known to correlate poorly with human judgment in short-answer QA tasks.
Grounding & Reliability Metrics (Citation Precision, Hallucination Rate): These measure the extent to which answers are verifiably supported by retrieved evidence. High citation precision and low hallucination reflect stronger factual grounding—a core goal of retrieval-augmented methods.

This multidimensional evaluation setup provides a holistic view of system behaviour, balancing correctness, clarity, and trustworthiness.

6	RESULTS
6.1 Accuracy and Semantic Alignment
Table 2a and Table 3a report accuracy and semantic alignment across subsets. On the dynamic subset, the hybrid model substantially outperforms extractive RAG and generative RAG with a frozen language model, achieving more than double the F1 and EM scores of FT-only and a large margin over retrieval-only baselines. This indicates that combining stable parametric reasoning with retrieved dynamic evidence enables effective generalization to previously unseen topics. On the stable subset, FT-only and hybrid models achieve comparable performance, reflecting the benefit of internalized knowledge when no domain shift is present.
Table 1: Evaluation Metrics Overview.
Metric	Description
Exact Match (EM)	Percentage of answers that exactly match the gold reference string.
F1 Score	Harmonic mean of token-level precision and recall between prediction and gold reference.
Semantic Similarity	Cosine similarity between sentence embeddings of prediction and gold answer (captures meaning beyond surface form).
BLEU	N-gram precision metric with brevity penalty; measures lexical overlap with gold reference.
ROUGE-L	Longest-common-subsequence recall; emphasizes summary-level alignment.
chrF	Character-level F-score combining precision and recall; robust to morphological variation.
Citation Precision	Fraction of retrieved or cited passages that actually support the generated claims.
Hallucination Rate	Proportion of statements not verifiable within the retrieved supports (unsupported claims).
