6.3 Grounding and Faithfulness
Citation precision and hallucination rate are computed via lexical overlap between generated answers and retrieved passages. As such, these metrics capture evidence usage rather than factual correctness per se, and may penalize correct answers generated from internalized knowledge.
Grounding and hallucination metrics are summarized in Tables 2c and 3c.
Extractive RAG achieves the lowest hallucination rate due to its retrieval-only design, which directly returns retrieved passages and therefore maximizes lexical overlap with supporting evidence.
In contrast, the hybrid model substantially reduces hallucinations compared to FT-only, particularly on dynamic questions, while exhibiting lower citation precision on stable topics.
This effect reflects the hybrid model’s reliance on parametric knowledge when answers are already internalized, reducing explicit reuse of retrieved context despite factual correctness.
6.4 Cross-Subset Trends
Across subsets, the hybrid approach exhibits the most balanced performance profile. While FT-only excels on stable questions and RAG-extractive and RAG-generative provide strong factual grounding, the hybrid model consistently bridges the gap between understanding and remembering. Performance on the mixed subset further highlights this trade-off, where hybrid models retain high fluency and accuracy while preserving reasonable grounding. On mixed questions, grounding metrics are inherently more challenging, as correct answers may combine internalized knowledge with selectively retrieved evidence, reducing lexical overlap with any single retrieved passage.
7	DISCUSSION
As demonstrated across Figures 3–6, the hybrid FT+RAG approach achieves the most balanced performance profile across accuracy, text quality, and factual reliability, outperforming both extractive retrieval-only and generative retrieval-augmented baselines.. Rather than optimizing a single dimension, the hybrid model effectively navigates the trade-off between parametric reasoning and retrieval-based grounding, yielding robust performance under dynamic conditions.
7.1	Understanding vs. Remembering
The results empirically support the cognitive distinction between “understanding” and “remembering.” Fine-tuning enables the model to internalize general reasoning patterns and linguistic structure, resulting in improved fluency and semantic alignment, but at the cost of increased hallucination when facing unfamiliar topics (Figure 5). In contrast, RAG-based systems exhibit strong grounding due to reliance on retrieved evidence, yet struggle with coherence and multi-hop reasoning (Figures 3–4). 
The hybrid model integrates these complementary capabilities, achieving the highest EM, F1, semantic similarity, and chrF scores while substantially reducing hallucinations relative to FT-only baselines.
Importantly, overlap-based grounding metrics may underestimate the faithfulness of hybrid models on stable topics, where correct answers are generated from internalized knowledge rather than explicit reuse of retrieved text.
 Figure 3: Accuracy Metrics (Dynamic Subset).
 Figure 4: Text Quality (Dynamic Subset).
7.2	Robustness Under Topic Shift
Our topic-disjoint evaluation setup introduces a semantically meaningful form of distribution shift, where models cannot rely on memorized facts and must instead generalize structural knowledge while incorporating new evidence. Under this challenging regime, the hybrid model demonstrates clear advantages, maintaining high accuracy and text quality on dynamic questions (Tables 2a–2b) and stable performance across subsets (Tables 3a–3c). These results suggest improved compositional generalization under unseen thematic contexts.
7.3	Practical Implications for Deployment
From a practical perspective, the hybrid architecture offers a flexible approach to knowledge maintenance. By separating stable knowledge—encoded via fine-tuning—from dynamic information accessed through retrieval, systems can adapt to evolving domains without repeated full retraining. While retrieval-based methods preserve grounding, integrating them with a fine-tuned backbone enables more coherent and contextually appropriate responses, making the hybrid approach well-suited for real-world QA systems operating over evolving knowledge bases.
 
Figure 5: Grounding and Reliability (Dynamic Subset).

 
Figure 6: Overall Comparison Summary (Dynamic Subset).
7.4	Limitations and Future Work
Despite its advantages, hybrid performance remains sensitive to retrieval quality, chunking strategy (e.g., 768 tokens with 16-token overlap in our setup), and alignment between retrieved context and generation. 
Future work should aim to formalize criteria for knowledge allocation: determining which information should be internalized through fine-tuning and which should remain externally retrievable. Developing data-driven heuristics for this partitioning could improve efficiency, robustness, and curriculum design for hybrid learners.
Additionally, future work should explore grounding metrics that distinguish factual correctness from lexical reuse, particularly in hybrid systems that combine parametric and non-parametric memory.
8	CONCLUSIONS
We presented a hybrid FT + RAG architecture that strategically divides knowledge between internalized stability and external adaptability.
Using a HotpotQA subset, the hybrid model achieved higher factual grounding and semantic richness than either fine-tuning alone or retrieval-only and generative RAG baselines.
This synergy between “understanding” and “remembering” demonstrates how small models can generalize beyond their training scope—mirroring human problem-solving that couples learned reasoning with external search.
